+### Contrast-based Quality Metric
+Contrast-based Quality Metric is a method to evaluate image quality for radial graph layout using contrast, defined as the standard deviation of pixel intensities [S4]. It calculates the mean pixel intensity $\mu$ for the image $\Omega$, then computes the standard deviation $\sigma$ of pixel intensities $v(i)$ around $\mu$ [S4]. The formula is $\sigma = \sqrt{ \frac{1}{|\Omega|} \sum_{i \in \Omega} (v(i) - \mu)^2 }$, where $\sigma$ is the contrast, $\Omega$ is the image, and $v(i)$ is the intensity of pixel $i$ [S4].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The computation is $O(m \cdot n)$ for all four metrics, as it requires a single pass (or two for Std) over the image pixels. Histogram computation is $O(m \cdot n)$ with $O(L)$ space. Gradient computation is $O(m \cdot n)$.
-*   **Performance:** The sources do not provide specific timing measurements. However, the simplicity of the operations (sums, squares, square roots) makes it computationally inexpensive compared to structural similarity or deep learning-based metrics.
